  "calculus": "ZX",
  "fragment": "phase-free",
  "group": "fault-equivalent",
  "order": 8,
  "summary": "A 2n-legged Z-spider is w-fault-equivalent to two n-legged spiders of which only w legs are connected pairwise.",
  "description": "A cheaper version of the [fault-equivalent spider ladder](../fe-fuse-n/): only the first $w$ legs of the two $n$-legged spiders pass through connected pairs of three-legged spiders, and the other $n-w$ legs are plain. The rewrite is not fault-equivalent but **$w$-fault-equivalent**: every undetectable fault of weight less than $w$ on one side has a fault of at most the same weight with the same effect on the other side. This suffices for instance for circuits that only need to be fault-tolerant up to distance $w$. Fault equivalence is with respect to *edge-flip noise*: every edge of the diagram can suffer a Pauli $X$, $Y$ or $Z$ flip of weight 1.",
  "conditions": "w-fault equivalence only; w ≥ 1 rungs.",
  "scalar": "1",
  "source": {
    "bib": "rodatz2025fault",
    "location": "Sec. 6.2, rule $(\\textsc{Fuse-n}_{w\\text{-fe}})$; proof in the appendix"
  },
